EXT. CANYON RIM - CAMP - DAWN

Red stone against a lemon sky. A thin column of smoke. Four bedrolls around a dead fire, a coffee pot on a flat rock. Horses hobbled in a stand of juniper.

A tall man sits cross-legged on a saddle, rolling a cigarette. ELIJAH VANCE-HOLLOWAY (40s, black hat, a face like a closed door). He licks the paper, tucks it behind his ear, and doesn't light it.

Beside him a lean woman scrapes a skillet clean with a knife. PERPETUA "PEPPER" DUNLEAVY (30s, braided hair, two pistols). She scrapes slowly, listening.

The third bedroll is empty. The fourth holds a boy of fifteen, sleeping with his boots on: TOBIAS MWANGI (15, freckles, too-big shirt). He snores.

At the edge of camp, a stooped man in a long grey duster watches the horizon with a spyglass: OLD AUGUSTUS FENWICK (60s, spectacles pushed up on his forehead).

AUGUSTUS
Smoke.

ELIJAH
Whose?

AUGUSTUS
South. Two miles.

ELIJAH
Ours or theirs?

AUGUSTUS
Don't know. Could be anybody's.

Pepper stops scraping. She sets the skillet down and wipes her knife on her trouser leg. She does not look at Elijah. He does not look at her.

PEPPER
We should wake the boy.

ELIJAH
Let him sleep.

PEPPER
He'll want to be awake for this.

ELIJAH
No, he won't.

Augustus lowers the spyglass. He turns it over in his hands, studies the brass, then screws it shut.

AUGUSTUS
Eleven riders.

ELIJAH
You said smoke.

AUGUSTUS
I said smoke. Then I counted.

Elijah draws the cigarette from behind his ear. Strikes a match on his boot. The flame trembles. He holds it a long time before lighting, as if the match were something to pray to.

ELIJAH
Eleven.

PEPPER
We have four.

ELIJAH
Three, with a child.

AUGUSTUS
Four.

ELIJAH
Four.

PEPPER
We could ride north.

ELIJAH
They'd see the dust.

PEPPER
We could ride now.

ELIJAH
They'd see the dust.

She nods, short, like a woman agreeing to be hanged. She stands, her two pistols clinking, and crosses to the juniper where the horses stand. She runs her hand along the nearest neck.

At the bedroll, the boy turns over and opens one eye.

TOBIAS
Is it morning?

ELIJAH
It's morning.

TOBIAS
Is there coffee?

ELIJAH
There will be.

Tobias sits up. Rubs his face. Sees Pepper at the horses. Sees Augustus with the spyglass. Sees the match burn down to Elijah's fingers.

TOBIAS
Who's coming?

Nobody answers. Elijah drops the match. It dies in the dust.

ELIJAH
Pepper. Saddle the boy's horse.

PEPPER
I've done it.

ELIJAH
Augustus. Water.

AUGUSTUS
I filled it last night.

ELIJAH
You always do.

He stands. Dusts his knees. Hands the unlit cigarette to Tobias, who takes it, astonished.

TOBIAS
What's this for?

ELIJAH
For after.

PEPPER
You don't say that to a boy.

ELIJAH
I'm saying it.

A distant sound: horses, many horses, a soft thunder rolling over the rim. Augustus puts the spyglass in his pocket. Pepper draws one pistol and spins it once on her finger. Tobias gets his boots under him.

TOBIAS
Can I have a gun?

ELIJAH
No.

PEPPER
Give him mine.

ELIJAH
Pepper.

PEPPER
He's got a right.

^TOBIAS
I've got a right.

They speak together, her voice dry, his voice cracking. Elijah looks from one to the other, then at the dust cloud boiling on the horizon, then at the coffee pot.

ELIJAH
(quietly)
Put on the coffee.

AUGUSTUS
Elijah...

ELIJAH
If we're going to die, we'll die warm.

Pepper sets the pot on the dead coals. Tobias snaps twigs into a pile. Augustus brings a match.

The thunder rolls closer. The twigs catch.

FADE OUT.
